        "original_id": "bdb88cfc-b4c0-5acf-9082-8a938d39c39a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: Manhattan Community Board 6, American-Scandinavian Foundation\n- Places: Gramercy Park, Stuyvesant Town, Peter Cooper Village, Waterside Plaza, Murray Hill, Kips Bay, Turtle Bay, Tudor City, Sutton Place, Manhattan, New York\n- Buildings: Tudor City, Scandinavia House – The Nordic Center in America\n- Topics: neighborhood boundaries, cultural centers, residential skyscraper complexes\n\nFacts:\n- In 1927, construction of Tudor City commenced, making Tudor City the first residential skyscraper complex in the world.\n- In 1999, Manhattan Community Board 6 defined Murray Hill's boundaries as East 34th Street to the south, East 40th Street to the north, Madison Avenue to the west, and the East River to the east.\n- Manhattan Community Board 6 includes the neighborhoods of Gramercy Park, Stuyvesant Town, Peter Cooper Village, Waterside Plaza, Murray Hill, Kips Bay, Turtle Bay, Tudor City, and Sutton Place.\n- Scandinavia House – The Nordic Center in America is the American-Scandinavian Foundation's cultural center at 58 Park Avenue between East 37th Street and East 38th Street in Murray Hill, Manhattan, New York.\n- Scandinavia House – The Nordic Center in America is dedicated to preserving the history of the Scandinavian and Nordic countries in the United States through exhibits and programming.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
